WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:07.280
How, then, do we slice the last four characters&nbsp;
of this text? Do you remember what we did in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:12.760
indexing topic to get something from the end&nbsp;
of a list? We used a minus sign. Let’s try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:20.760
that here. I will copy the code from line two&nbsp;
to line four, then make the first part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:29.160
slice a minus four and delete the text after the&nbsp;
colon. This iteration of a slice is saying slice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:37.800
four characters, starting at the end of the text.&nbsp;
I will rerun the code, and the last result brings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:45.880
us the four characters at the end of the serial&nbsp;
number. Before we go any further, you may wonder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:51.400
why is the number before the colon? Here’s what&nbsp;
I think to remember this, compared to the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:59.440
slice. This slice mirrors this slice because&nbsp;
we have a negative number here. Think of it as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:06.360
starting from the end, the right side, and working&nbsp;
back four characters. The last type of slice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:11.720
to slice characters in the middle of a value,&nbsp;
may take a little practice. We want to slice the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:21.000
characters in the middle, 2222-3333. I will start&nbsp;
another print statement for the serial number on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:27.160
line 5 and add the square brackets in anticipation&nbsp;
of the slice. What character do you think we need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:39.120
to start with? Remember, Python is zero-based,&nbsp;
so if we count 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, we need to start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:47.400
with character 5, the first two. The number after&nbsp;
the colon represents the character number to end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:54.960
the slice. All you need to figure out is this:&nbsp;
how many characters do we need? We have eight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:02.440
numbers and a dash, making nine characters.&nbsp;
We need to add nine to the first number, five,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:13.720
to get 14. I will rerun this code, and the last&nbsp;
result gives us the characters we want. Rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:19.080
than try to count what character you need for&nbsp;
the ending point, add the number you need to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:25.200
first number in the slice, and you can easily&nbsp;
slice characters out of the middle of a value.

